        rules: {
          objective: "Arrange 13 cards into three poker hands (back: 5 cards, middle: 5 cards, front: 3 cards) where back ≥ middle ≥ front in strength. Win units by beating opponents' corresponding hands.",
          gameplay: 'Each player receives 13 cards and arranges them into three hands face down: back hand (5 cards, strongest), middle hand (5 cards, middle strength), front hand (3 cards, weakest). Back must beat middle, middle must beat front. Players expose hands simultaneously. Each pair of players compares corresponding hands, winning one unit per hand beaten.',
          keyRules: [
            '13 cards dealt to each player',
            'Arrange into back (5), middle (5), front (3) hands',
            'Back hand must be strongest, then middle, then front',
            'Standard poker hand rankings apply',
            'Front hand: only 3-of-a-kind, pair, or high card possible',
            "Straights and flushes don't count in front hand",
            'Win 1 unit per hand beaten against each opponent',
            'Bonus payments for strong hands',
            'Special 13-card hands win automatically',
            'Foul (mis-set) hands lose all three hands to all opponents',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Ensure your hands are legal (back ≥ middle ≥ front). Put your strongest hand in back, weakest in front. Don't risk fouling by making front too strong.",
          intermediate: "Balance hand strength across all three positions. Consider that front hand only needs to beat other front hands - don't over-commit strong cards there. Look for opportunities to make three of a kind in front for bonus. Manage risk of fouling versus potential rewards.",
          advanced: "Sophisticated hand arrangement considering probabilities of opponents' holdings. Optimal distribution of pairs and high cards. Recognizing when to play for sweep (winning all three hands). Understanding expected value of different arrangements. In Open Face, tracking exposed cards and adjusting strategy.",
          tips: [
            'Never foul - ensure back ≥ middle ≥ front',
            'Front hand only needs to beat other fronts - conserve strength',
            'Three of a kind in front is valuable (3 units)',
            'Consider probability of opponents having certain hands',
            'Balance risk of strong middle versus weak back',
            'Special hands (six pairs, three straights) are automatic winners',
            "In Open Face, watch opponents' exposed cards carefully",
